1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:10,530
Pulse concepts. The building blocks of a request, explained on one small cohort of twelve members.

2
00:00:11,330 --> 00:00:17,461
Everything in Pulse starts with a cohort: a table of records, where every field has a declared type.

3
00:00:18,261 --> 00:00:29,981
Field types today cover whole numbers, decimals, dates, true-or-false flags, categories, multi-select sets and native vectors. More are added over time, and the manifest lists them.

4
00:00:30,781 --> 00:00:40,581
A field can be null if its schema allows it, meaning no value at all. For a multi-select, an empty selection is a real answer, which is different from null.

5
00:00:41,381 --> 00:00:47,981
A request is a recipe. You fill in the slots you need, and by default Pulse runs them in the same order.

6
00:00:48,781 --> 00:00:59,840
Features first, then filters, attributes, groups, aggregations, windows, and finally sort. A request that groups or aggregates a window's output runs the windows first.

7
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:06,877
Around that spine sit tests, regressions, matrices, crosstabs and overlays. Let's meet each one.

8
00:01:07,677 --> 00:01:13,765
Features come first, before any filter. Each one adds a new column to every record in the cohort.

9
00:01:14,565 --> 00:01:19,992
A log transform pulls in a long tail. Bucketize turns age into numbered bands.

10
00:01:20,792 --> 00:01:29,483
More feature operators are added over time. Because features run first, they see all twelve rows, even the ones a filter is about to drop.

11
00:01:30,283 --> 00:01:35,880
Filters decide which records stay. This one keeps members aged eighteen to sixty-five.

12
00:01:36,680 --> 00:01:43,685
Three records fall away. The filter counts what it saw and what it let through, and those counts come back with the answer.

13
00:01:44,485 --> 00:01:55,757
Filters range from simple ranges to set membership to full expressions, and more are added over time. A filter can read features, and by default it runs before the attributes.

14
00:01:56,557 --> 00:02:05,482
Attributes add a derived value to each record, by default after the filters. A formula reads only its own row, so it needs one pass.

15
00:02:06,282 --> 00:02:13,117
A z-score is different. It needs the mean and spread of the whole column first, so Pulse makes a pass to learn them.

16
00:02:13,917 --> 00:02:20,538
Then a second pass scores every row. Member seven has no income, so its z-score is null, not zero.

17
00:02:21,338 --> 00:02:25,634
Pulse handles both passes for you, and the set of attributes keeps growing.

18
00:02:26,434 --> 00:02:31,519
Groupers sort records into buckets. The category grouper makes one bucket per region.

19
00:02:32,319 --> 00:02:38,023
Multi-select fields can fan out. A member who chose travel and tech lands in both buckets.

20
00:02:38,823 --> 00:02:49,114
Groupers cover categories, number ranges, quantiles, calendar periods, clusters and sets, with more added over time. Two groupers together make a combined key.

21
00:02:49,914 --> 00:02:57,538
Aggregators collapse each bucket into one number. Here, the average income per region becomes one row of the result.

22
00:02:58,338 --> 00:03:05,642
Each figure comes with its counts. In the East, one income was null, so that average stands on two records, not three.

23
00:03:06,442 --> 00:03:13,554
Pulse ships a growing set of aggregators. And with no groupers at all, the whole filtered cohort is a single bucket.

24
00:03:14,354 --> 00:03:21,914
By default, windows run after aggregation. They give each row a value that depends on its neighbours, in a set order.

25
00:03:22,714 --> 00:03:30,061
Lag pulls in last month's revenue. Percent change compares against it. A running sum keeps a cumulative total.

26
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:38,805
Windows also include ranks, moving averages, smoothing, shares, lift and carry-over lags, and more are added over time.

27
00:03:39,605 --> 00:03:48,253
Tests ask whether a difference is real or just noise. Here, a filter keeps two regions, and a Welch t-test compares their incomes.

28
00:03:49,053 --> 00:03:56,314
Pulse offers a growing set of tests. Some run during the scan, and post-tests run on the finished result rows.

29
00:03:57,114 --> 00:04:03,949
Regressions explain an outcome from its predictors. A least-squares fit finds how income rises with age.

30
00:04:04,749 --> 00:04:16,853
Regressions today include least squares, G L Ms, Bayesian linear and media-mix models. More are added over time, and embedders can register their own through options dot extensions.

31
00:04:17,653 --> 00:04:25,277
Matrices look at many fields at once. You name a vector of fields, or a native vector field, and get a whole matrix back.

32
00:04:26,077 --> 00:04:37,669
Correlation shows which fields move together. Other matrix operators include covariance, principal components, reliability and structural equation models, with more added over time.

33
00:04:38,469 --> 00:04:45,453
Overlays are layers placed on top of a finished result. A share-of-total layer lines up with the regional rows.

34
00:04:46,253 --> 00:04:51,317
The base data underneath never changes. An overlay only adds a sibling layer.

35
00:04:52,117 --> 00:04:57,288
Overlays work on series too. Delta versus prior shows the month-on-month change.

36
00:04:58,088 --> 00:05:05,989
A crosstab, a matrix result, a facet or a chain can host overlays as well, and new overlay kinds are added over time.

37
00:05:06,789 --> 00:05:21,218
That is the whole toolkit: a spine of seven slots in a default order, with tests, regressions, matrices, crosstabs and overlays around it. Every category grows over time, and the manifest lists what your instance offers.

38
00:05:22,018 --> 00:05:27,914
In most categories, embedders can register their own operators through options dot extensions.

39
00:05:28,714 --> 00:05:34,311
And every figure comes back with the counts it stands on, so you can always ask: out of how many?

40
00:05:35,111 --> 00:05:38,639
Next, we'll take each of these concepts apart, in depth.
